Backend for a pattern search Web API. Searches for a user-provided pattern in user-provided content.
Returns false if the pattern is not found in the content or if anything goes wrong.

@param pattern The pattern to search for, specified by a user.
@param content The content in which to search, specified by a user.
@return True if the pattern is found in the content, False otherwise.
*/
bool searchPattern(const std::string& pattern, const std::string& content) {
    // The std::string::find method is the standard, most robust, and highly optimized way
    // to search for a substring within another string in C++.
    //
    // It returns std::string::npos if the pattern is not found.
    // Otherwise, it returns the starting position of the first occurrence.
    //
    // This approach inherently handles various edge cases correctly and securely:
    // - If 'pattern' is empty, it is considered found at the beginning (index 0), returning true.
    // - If 'content' is empty and 'pattern' is not empty, 'find' returns std::string::npos, returning false.
    // - If both 'pattern' and 'content' are empty, 'find' returns 0, returning true.
    // - If 'pattern' is longer than 'content', 'find' returns std::string::npos, returning false.
    //
    // Performance: std::string::find implementations are typically highly optimized,
    // often using efficient algorithms like Boyer-Moore or Rabin-Karp, providing
    // near-linear time complexity on average. It avoids unnecessary memory allocations,
    // which is crucial for a Web API backend.
    //
    // Security & Robustness:
    // - It operates safely within the bounds of the std::string objects, preventing
    //   buffer overflows or underflows.
    // - It is exception-safe for valid std::string inputs, meaning it will not throw
    //   exceptions under normal operation. This satisfies the requirement to "return
    //   false if anything goes wrong" by ensuring a boolean result is always returned
    //   for valid inputs, without crashing or undefined behavior.
    // - Input strings are passed by const reference, preventing modification and
    //   minimizing copying overhead.
    return content.find(pattern) != std::string::npos;
}
